title: The Friesland Affair
subtitle: The general secretary turns on Moscow.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1922 and month = 1
max-visits: 1

= The Friesland Affair

Ernst Reuter, who writes as Friesland, was general secretary of the party and one of the loudest voices for the March offensive. Now he says the party is run by envoys from Moscow, that the Zentrale hides where its money comes from, and that German workers will never follow a party that cannot decide for itself. [? if levi_split = 1 : Levi said the same a year ago, and Moscow wants the same answer: expulsion. ?]

Half the Central Committee wants him out by the end of the month. The other half has read his pamphlet and agrees with parts of it.

- @expel: Expel him.
- @keep: Keep him, and let the party argue it out.

@expel
subtitle: Moscow is satisfied. He takes some of Berlin with him.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('zinoviev', 6);
RG.district('berlin', {members: -0.04, loyalty: 3});
Q.e22_friesland = 1;
Q.independence -= 5;
!}

The Central Committee votes him out. Reuter takes a few hundred Berlin members with him[? if kag_exists = 1 : , first to Levi's Working Group and then to the SPD ?].

@keep
subtitle: The party gains a voice of its own, and Moscow's money comes late. He may not stay.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('zinoviev', -6);
RG.ecci('radek', -3);
Q.e22_friesland = 2;
Q.independence += 8;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 1;
Q.moscow_subsidy = Math.max(0, Q.moscow_subsidy - 1);
!}

For two months *Die Rote Fahne* prints letters for and against. Reuter keeps his card. Radek writes home that the German party has caught Levi's disease.
